+S20 P3 — bidirectionality verification log (ticket #102, epic #98)
+Frozen contract: features/propose-grows-up/p3-brainstorm.md (v11)
+
+METHOD
+------
+A test that passes while the code is present proves nothing on its own — it
+may be asserting something the code does not decide. So every production
+behavior this phase adds was verified in BOTH directions: green with the code
+present, and RED with the code deliberately broken.
+
+For each behavior below the harness, run from a clean tree at HEAD:
+  1. asserts the mutation string occurs EXACTLY ONCE in the target file
+     (so the break is precise and cannot silently miss);
+  2. applies that one mutation;
+  3. runs the naming test file under vitest and records the genuine failing
+     test names, verbatim;
+  4. restores with `git checkout -- <file>`.
+After the last entry the whole suite is re-run and `git status` is asserted
+clean, so nothing below leaked into the tree.
+
+The harness is reproducible: scratchpad/bidi.sh + scratchpad/mutate.py.
+
+RESULT: 37 behaviors exercised — 35 are PINNED (breaking them fails at least
+one named test) and 2 are CONFIRMED BEHAVIOURAL NO-OPS, both recorded with
+their proofs below rather than given a phantom assertion.
+
+TWO CONFIRMED NO-OPS (deliberate, not gaps)
+-------------------------------------------
+(1) SEAM :441 — `recipeLabel`'s catalog argument in the CONSTRAINED row.
+    The frozen spec predicts this and forbids a pin for it; the sweep
+    confirms the prediction empirically. `recipeLabel` uses its catalog for
+    one thing, `dflt = effectiveDefaultRecipe(catalog, …)`, and that site
+    executes only inside `constrainedRaws.map`. A row is constrained exactly
+    when `effectiveDefaultRecipe(gated, …) === null`, so under correct wiring
+    no option can carry `(default)`. Under the missed edit `dflt` is the
+    ungated default D — but constrained implies D is gated out, and the
+    options come from `producerRecipesFor(gated, …)`, so D is absent from
+    them and the tag still never fires. The site is changed for consistency;
+    the `(default)` tag is pinned where it CAN fire, on a normal stage row.
+
+(2) The TIER select's `value` binding
+    (`tierOptions.includes(t) ? String(t) : ""`).
+    This one departs from the v11 amendment, which asked for it to be pinned
+    in jsdom on `select.value === ""`. MEASURED: that assertion cannot
+    discriminate, and neither can any other client-DOM assertion. Rendering
+    the same select with `value=""` and with `value="999"` (no matching
+    option) yields a byte-identical DOM — value "", selectedIndex 0,
+    per-option `selected` flags [true,false,false], and identical innerHTML —
+    because the DOM's own "ask for a reset" algorithm selects the first
+    option when none matches, and that first option IS "all". The difference
+    the amendment reasoned about is real only in React's SERVER renderer,
+    which emits `selected` attributes; this app is a client-rendered SPA and
+    never takes that path. The explicit binding is kept (it is the specced
+    shape and keeps a server-rendered string honest) and the user-visible
+    outcome — an above-range tier displays "all", with no write-back — IS
+    pinned; only the binding expression is unpinnable, and no test pretends
+    otherwise.
